<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#daric">Glossary</a> · Term · currency</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daric</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The daric was a gold coin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, introduced under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>. Of consistently high purity, it served as a prestigious medium of exchange and a tool of royal policy, used to pay troops and to subsidise allies and clients, including Greek states. Its standardised issue reflected the fiscal organisation of the Persian state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daric.jpg" alt="Daric" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AchaemenidDaric4thCenturyBCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Coinage reached the Persian Empire after 546 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and defeated its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>, who had put in place the first coinage in history. Cyrus seems at first to have adopted the Lydian coinage as it stood and continued to strike Lydia's lion-and-bull coins. The daric was introduced later by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>, who reigned from 521 to 486 BCE, as a new thick gold coin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The daric had a standard weight of 8.4 grams and equalled in value 20 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, the sigloi, so that the darics and sigloi together represented the bimetallic standard of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. The gold was of very high quality, with a purity of 95.83 per cent. The coins bore the image of the Persian king or a great warrior armed with a bow and arrow, and the numismatic evidence allows the figure to be identified only as the king, whom Darius adopted on his coins as an expression of royal power.</p><p>The right of coinage was an exclusively royal prerogative. Near the end of the 5th century BCE the Persian satraps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> decided to strike their own coins, which Darius treated as a crime punishable by death. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> believed that the Greek word dareikos derived from the name of Darius. Some scholars agree, reconstructing an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> word *dārayaka-, while others trace it to Old Persian *dari-, meaning golden, and think the link with Darius arose later as folk etymology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> mentions the coin twice, as adarkonim, because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> met it when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> conquered their Babylonian conquerors; the First Book of Chronicles lists darics among gifts for the Temple, in a passage set in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s time, so the term is either an anachronism or the writer's conversion into contemporary units. After bribes paid by a Persian satrap started the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a>, the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agesilaus-ii">Agesilaus II</a> was recalled from a successful campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and remarked that thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, meaning the figure on the coin, had driven him out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Use of the daric ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s invasion in 330 BCE, and most darics were melted down and recoined as Alexander's coins.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daric” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The gold <b>daric</b> and silver siglos gave a common royal coinage to an empire that reached from the Indus to the Aegean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
